Uploading in GitHub (often referred to as pushing code) is the process of sending your local project files, code, 
and folders from your computer to a remote repository hosted on GitHub. 
This allows you to safely back up your work, track changes over time, and collaborate with other developers.
1. Using the GitHub Web Interface (Easiest for Beginners)
If you have a small project or a few files, you can upload them directly through your web browser without using any code tools
 Using Git and the Command Line (Standard for Developers)
For larger projects and regular updates, developers install a tool called Git on their computers to upload files using terminal commands:
Step	Command	What It Does
1. Initialize	git init	Prepares your local project folder to start tracking changes.
2. Stage	git add .	Gathers all the files in your folder, preparing them to be saved.
3. Commit	git commit -m "My first upload"	Permanently saves a snapshot of the files on your local computer.
4. Link	git remote add origin <URL>	Links your local folder to your empty GitHub repository link.
5. Push	git push -u origin main	Uploads the committed files straight to your GitHub profile.
